$NetBSD$

Turn ASLR off for temacs and for the dumped emacs.

This is upstream pkgsrc's change, carried since the package was imported; why
it was needed is not recorded, and editors/emacs20 is the only emacs in the
tree that does it.  The usual reason given for it is that unexec writes the
running image back out as an executable, so the addresses it recorded have to
be the addresses it gets next time, and NetBSD randomises them by default
(security.pax.aslr.global = 1).

That reason is not one I could reproduce.  On NetBSD 11.0/amd64 an unexec'd
emacs 21.4.1 with no PaX flag started 500 times out of 500 without a fault,
and so did the same binary with the flag set.  So the flag is kept because it
is what the package has always done and it costs nothing, not because the
failure it guards against has been seen here.  i386, where the address space
is tight enough for randomisation to matter more, was not tested.

--- src/Makefile.in.orig	2016-11-26 06:25:26.875919180 +0000
+++ src/Makefile.in
@@ -823,6 +816,7 @@ emacs: temacs ${etc}DOC ${lisp}
 #else /* ! defined (HAVE_SHM) */
 	./temacs -batch -l loadup dump
 #endif /* ! defined (HAVE_SHM) */
+	/usr/sbin/paxctl +a emacs || true
 #endif /* ! defined (CANNOT_DUMP) */
 	-./emacs -q -batch -f list-load-path-shadows
 
@@ -851,6 +845,7 @@ temacs: MAKE_PARALLEL $(LOCALCPP) $(STAR
 	$(LD) YMF_PASS_LDFLAGS (${STARTFLAGS} ${ALL_LDFLAGS}) \
     -o temacs ${STARTFILES} ${obj} ${otherobj}  \
     OBJECTS_SYSTEM OBJECTS_MACHINE ${LIBES}
+	/usr/sbin/paxctl +a temacs || true
 
 /* We don't use ALL_LDFLAGS because LD_SWITCH_SYSTEM and LD_SWITCH_MACHINE
    often contain options that have to do with using Emacs's crt0,
